"Student activity · Physics I foundations",
"<strong>Tabs in the demo:</strong> Scale of things; Convert units; Dimensional analysis; Significant figures; Fermi estimates. This handout uses the last two; the first three go with the Measuring, Converting and Checking Units handout. The page opens on Scale of things, so choose the tab you need.",
"<strong>Significant figures</strong>: enter two measurements as you would write them (the tab opens with {#} × {#}) and choose ×, ÷, + or −. The table gives each number’s significant figures and decimal places, and the line below gives the calculator value, the rule applied (keep so many significant figures, or so many decimal places) and the rounded result. A decimal point makes trailing zeros significant; {#} with no decimal point is ambiguous and is taken as {#} figures here.",
"<strong>Fermi estimates</strong>: choose a <strong>Question</strong> (<strong>Heartbeats</strong>, <strong>Walk the equator</strong> or <strong>Air per day</strong>). Each factor has a low and a high value that you adjust to your own estimates; the demo opens with typical values, such as {#} to {#} heartbeats per minute and {#} to {#} years in a lifetime, and marks exact factors like {#} minutes per day. The answer is the product, given as a range and to the nearest power of ten.",
"There is no Record or Export in this demo: the Data table is filled from your own estimates and the supplied exercise.",
